{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788127485,"nonce":"1iYQUePrSESOnSa69tqMFVLKsyQ=","message":"RESOLVED by Dorian — fog of war, and the blocker taxonomy. Session 3.\nBoth are now in DESIGN.md's decisions log; this comment is the working detail.\n\n\nEvery observer and query hides undiscovered cells — `map-view`, `find-rect`,\n`nearest`, `room-at` alike. One rule, mirroring the action model's player/dev\nsplit, so it cannot drift into three behaviours the way 2.3 did (`nearest`\nskipped fogged things, `map-view` rendered full detail under fog, `find-rect`\nwould return \"buildable\" candidates in unexplored ground).\n\nTwo reasons it went this way: the agent must not site a building in ground it\nhas never explored, and an agent holding information no player has weakens the\ncolony as a test substrate — exploration-triggered mod bugs would never fire.\n\n`c.Fogged(map)` is the test. In `map-view`, fogged cells get their own glyph\nrather than being blanked, so the agent can see the SHAPE of what it has not\nexplored — that is real information a player has too.\n\n\nDorian's point, and it is the concrete form of the standing \"candidates +\nreasons, never bare booleans\" invariant: some obstacles are mined, some\ndeconstructed, and some have to be beaten down by a drafted colonist. A bare\n`buildable: false` cannot be acted on.\n\n**Do not invent a taxonomy — the game already has one.** Verified in the\ndecompiled 1.6 source:\n\n- `Building.DeconstructibleBy(Faction)` (`Verse/Building.cs:488`) returns an\n  `AcceptanceReport` — accepted for your own faction's buildings, for\n  `def.building.alwaysDeconstructible`, and for comps answering\n  `CompForceDeconstructable()`; rejected when `!def.building.IsDeconstructible`.\n  The report carries the game's own reason string.\n- `ThingDef.IsNonDeconstructibleAttackableBuilding` (`Verse/ThingDef.cs:1303`)\n  is `IsBuildingArtificial \u0026\u0026 !building.IsDeconstructible \u0026\u0026 destroyable \u0026\u0026\n  !mineable \u0026\u0026 building.isTargetable \u0026\u0026 building.draftAttackNonDeconstructable`.\n- `Designator_Deconstruct.CanDesignateThing` (`:94-99`) already answers that\n  exact case with the literal string `RemoveByAttackingTooltip` — \"remove this\n  by attacking it\".\n\nSo every spatial result that rejects a cell or thing carries:\n\n    \"removal\": \"mine\" | \"deconstruct\" | \"attack\" | \"none\"\n    \"reason\":  \u003cthe game's own AcceptanceReport string, verbatim\u003e\n\n- `mine` — `def.mineable`. Cleared by 3.2 `designate mine`. (Mountains, ore.)\n- `deconstruct` — `DeconstructibleBy(Faction.OfPlayer)` accepted. Cleared by\n  3.2 `designate deconstruct`.\n- `attack` — `def.IsNonDeconstructibleAttackableBuilding`. Cleared by 3.4:\n  draft a colonist and attack the thing. Hostile-faction and mech-cluster\n  structures land here.\n- `none` — none of the above; genuinely permanent. Plan around it.\n\nWhere this lands: **2.6** is already rewriting `find-rect`'s candidate path, so\nthe `removal` field goes in there. **3.2** owns mine/deconstruct, **3.4** owns\nthe drafted attack, and **3.3**'s `place-layout` preflight should report the\nper-cell failures in the same shape so the agent can decide between \"clear this\nand retry\" and \"site it elsewhere\".","files":null}]}